  <h1>Prediction Models in Trading</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Why this matters for <strong>HFT_practice</strong>: Lesson 0003 covered the
    <em>algorithms</em> in the repo (linear regression, MA rule, gradient boosting).
    This lesson zooms out to the <em>design space</em> those algorithms sit inside.
    Every working trading model is a five-axis decision; understanding the axes lets
    you evaluate, replace, or extend what's here without flailing.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §1 FIVE AXES ===================== -->
  <h2>1. A trading prediction model lives on five axes</h2>
  <p>
    A "model" in trading isn't just "GBM" or "LSTM." Two engineers can both say
    "gradient boosting" and have built completely different systems. The thing
    that actually differs is these five choices:
  </p>

  <div class="axis"><strong>1. Target</strong> — what number (or class) you train to predict.</div>
  <div class="axis"><strong>2. Horizon</strong> — over what future window the target is measured.</div>
  <div class="axis"><strong>3. Pooling</strong> — one model across all assets, or one per asset.</div>
  <div class="axis"><strong>4. Output shape</strong> — point estimate, distribution, or action.</div>
  <div class="axis"><strong>5. Adaptivity</strong> — train once forever, or rolling retrain.</div>

  <p>Where this repo sits:</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Axis</th><th>This repo's choice</th><th>Where it's set</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Target</td><td>Next-day return magnitude (a continuous number)</td><td><code>build_features</code> → <code>fwd_ret_1d</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Horizon</td><td>1 trading day</td><td><code>.shift(-1)</code> in <code>build_features</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pooling</td><td><strong>Cross-sectional</strong> — one model on stacked rows from all tickers</td><td><code>simulate</code> in <code>paper_trading_sim.py</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Output shape</td><td>Point estimate (no uncertainty, no quantiles)</td><td><code>GradientBoostingRegressor.predict</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Adaptivity</td><td>Either: <em>one-shot train</em> (paper_trading_sim) or <em>rolling retrain</em> (walkforward_sim with <code>--rolling_retrain</code>)</td><td><code>walkforward_sim.py:184–207</code></td></tr>
  </table>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>The mental shift:</strong> "What model should I use?" is the wrong opening question.
    "What target, on what horizon, pooled how, expressed how, refreshed how often?" — answer those,
    and the algorithm choice (GBM, linear, neural) follows almost mechanically.
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §2 TARGET ===================== -->
  <h2>2. Axis 1 — What you predict</h2>

  <p>
    The single most consequential modeling choice. The same dataset can support
    several different prediction problems, each with different math, different
    evaluation, and different downstream use.
  </p>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Target</th><th>Type</th><th>Why pick it</th><th>Used in this repo?</th></tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Return direction</strong> (up/down)</td>
      <td>Binary classification</td>
      <td>You only care about side of the bet.</td>
      <td><em>No</em> — would lose magnitude info needed for ranking.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Return magnitude</strong> (a number)</td>
      <td>Regression</td>
      <td>You need to rank N tickers and size positions.</td>
      <td><strong>Yes</strong>. <code>fwd_ret_1d</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Volatility</strong> (next-day or next-week)</td>
      <td>Regression (positive)</td>
      <td>Sizing positions, pricing options, risk overlays.</td>
      <td><em>Not directly</em>, but <code>vol_10</code> shows up as a feature.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Jump probability</strong></td>
      <td>Binary classification on a rare event</td>
      <td>Tail-risk hedging, event-driven strategies.</td>
      <td>No.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Market regime</strong> (calm / stress)</td>
      <td>Hidden state classification</td>
      <td>Switch strategy or reduce exposure under stress.</td>
      <td><strong>Yes, secondary</strong>. GaussianMixture in <code>walkforward_sim.py</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Optimal action</strong> (buy/hold/sell amount)</td>
      <td>Reinforcement learning</td>
      <td>End-to-end optimization including execution.</td>
      <td>No. Out of scope.</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>What about horizon?</h3>
  <p>
    Horizon and target are tangled. <em>"Next return"</em> means nothing without a
    horizon. The repo's daily horizon shapes everything: yfinance OHLCV is daily,
    features are daily, target is 1-day-ahead, retraining (when enabled) is monthly.
    Move to a 5-second horizon and you'd need tick data, microsecond features,
    and a completely different infrastructure (see Lessons 0001–0002).
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §3 POOLING ===================== -->
  <h2>3. Axis 3 — Cross-sectional pooling, the non-obvious decision</h2>

  <p>
    The repo trains <strong>one</strong> <code>GradientBoostingRegressor</code> on
    stacked rows from every ticker. This is a meaningful choice, and many beginners
    would default to the other approach without realizing they've picked.
  </p>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Per-asset models (one per ticker)</h4>
      <p>
        Train AAPL's model only on AAPL data, MSFT's only on MSFT data, etc.
        Captures asset-specific quirks; suffers from small training sets;
        completely fails on a ticker with no history (IPOs, additions to the index).
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Cross-sectional pooled (this repo)</h4>
      <p>
        Stack all (ticker, date) rows into one big dataset; train one model that
        learns a feature-to-return mapping that applies to <em>any</em> ticker.
        Far more training data; generalizes to new tickers; assumes the
        feature-return relationship is roughly universal.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <span class="filepath">paper_trading_sim.py:simulate (lines 198–216)</span>
<pre>stacked = []
<span class="kw">for</span> t, f <span class="kw">in</span> feats.items():
    f2 = f.<span class="fn">copy</span>()
    f2[<span class="str">"ticker"</span>] = t                   <span class="com"># label the ticker on each row</span>
    stacked.<span class="fn">append</span>(f2)
panel = pd.<span class="fn">concat</span>(stacked).<span class="fn">dropna</span>()  <span class="com"># one big tall DataFrame</span>

<span class="com"># ... time-split each ticker ...</span>

X_train, y_train, feat_cols = <span class="fn">prepare_xy</span>(train_df)
model = <span class="fn">train_model</span>(X_train, y_train)    <span class="com"># single model, all tickers' rows</span>
</pre>

  <p>
    Notice <code>prepare_xy</code> explicitly drops the <code>ticker</code> column from
    the feature matrix. The model never learns "AAPL behaves differently than MSFT" —
    it learns a universal mapping from technical features to return. That's the
    cross-sectional assumption baked in.
  </p>

  <div class="callout warn">
    <strong>When pooling breaks:</strong> if a small-cap biotech behaves nothing like
    a mega-cap tech name, a pooled model averages across the difference and gets both
    wrong. Real production setups often pool <em>within</em> sector or market-cap
    bucket. The repo doesn't bucket at all — keep that limit in mind.
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §4 FAMILIES ===================== -->
  <h2>4. Axis-blind tour: the model families you'll see in trading literature</h2>

  <div class="grid3">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Linear / factor models</h4>
      <p>
        Fama-French, BARRA, Arbitrage Pricing Theory. Weights on a small set of
        risk factors (size, value, momentum). Still dominant in mid-frequency.
        FPGA-friendly. <strong>In repo:</strong> the predictor in
        <code>linear_regression_predictor.vhd</code> is the simplest case.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Tree ensembles</h4>
      <p>
        Gradient boosting (XGBoost, LightGBM, sklearn GBM), random forests. Workhorse
        of buy-side equity research at the daily/weekly horizon.
        Non-linear, robust, easy to validate. <strong>In repo:</strong> the flagship.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Sequence neural nets</h4>
      <p>
        LSTM / GRU / Transformer applied to OHLCV sequences. Capture long-range
        temporal dependencies. Heavy to train, hard to backtest honestly. Common
        in academic papers, less in production than you'd think.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Time-series foundation models (2024–2025 wave)</h4>
      <p>
        TimesFM (Google, 200M params), Chronos (Amazon, T5-based), Kronos
        (pretrained on K-line data). Pretrained on billions of time-series points;
        used for zero-/few-shot forecasting. Promising; far from outperforming
        well-tuned trees on tabular alpha.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>State-space / regime models</h4>
      <p>
        Hidden Markov Models, Gaussian Mixture Models. Don't predict returns
        directly; identify a latent regime (calm/stress) and route policy.
        <strong>In repo:</strong> <code>walkforward_sim.py</code> uses
        <code>GaussianMixture</code> as a stress detector.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Reinforcement learning</h4>
      <p>
        Learn an action policy directly from reward signals. Promising for
        execution and market-making. Notoriously hard to train and validate
        on financial data. Not in this repo.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>The honest takeaway</h3>
  <p>
    For tabular daily-frequency equity prediction — exactly what this repo does — a
    well-tuned gradient booster beats almost everything. Neural sequence models and
    foundation models win on text-like or richly-sequential inputs, not on a few
    daily-bar features. The repo's choice of GBM is defensible, even with all the
    sexier options on the table.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §5 IN THIS PROJECT ===================== -->
  <h2>5. In this project — the adaptivity axis</h2>

  <p>
    Of the five axes, the one Lesson 0003 didn't touch is <strong>adaptivity</strong>.
    The repo actually has both modes wired up — fair to know they exist.
  </p>

  <h3>Mode A: train-once (the simple backtest)</h3>
  <span class="filepath">paper_trading_sim.py:simulate (overview)</span>
<pre><span class="com"># 1. Take 70% of the timeline as training (default --train_ratio=0.7).</span>
<span class="com"># 2. Fit ONE model on that block.</span>
<span class="com"># 3. Use it unchanged for the entire 30% test block.</span>
model = <span class="fn">train_model</span>(X_train, y_train)
preds = model.<span class="fn">predict</span>(X_test)
</pre>
  <p>
    Simple to reason about, but the model never sees the regime shift between 2019
    and 2024. A pre-COVID model "predicting" 2020 is, charitably, optimistic.
  </p>

  <h3>Mode B: rolling retrain</h3>
  <span class="filepath">walkforward_sim.py (lines 184–235, condensed)</span>
<pre><span class="kw">if</span> rolling_retrain <span class="kw">and</span> <span class="fn">_should_retrain</span>(last_train_dt, dt):
    <span class="com"># Re-fit the model using all rows up to (but not including) `dt`</span>
    <span class="com"># Frequency: --retrain_freq {daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly}</span>
    <span class="com"># Window:    --train_window_days N  (e.g. 756 = ~3y)</span>
    model = <span class="fn">refit_model</span>(history_up_to(dt))
    last_train_dt = dt
</pre>
  <p>
    Every retrain bin (default monthly), the model is refit on the most recent
    <code>--train_window_days</code> of past data only. Each prediction uses information
    that was actually available at the time — the textbook walk-forward discipline
    that prevents look-ahead bias.
  </p>

  <h3>Mode C (bolted on): a regime model as risk overlay</h3>
  <span class="filepath">walkforward_sim.py (lines 300–317)</span>
<pre><span class="com"># GaussianMixture as a stress/regime classifier on recent returns</span>
gm = <span class="fn">GaussianMixture</span>(n_components=hmm_states, random_state=<span class="num">42</span>)
gm.<span class="fn">fit</span>(returns_window)
probs = gm.<span class="fn">predict_proba</span>(returns_window)

<span class="kw">if</span> probs[-<span class="num">1</span>, stress_comp] >= hmm_prob:
    gm_hit = <span class="kw">True</span>          <span class="com"># "today looks like the high-vol component"</span>

stress = <span class="fn">bool</span>(high_vol <span class="kw">or</span> deep_dd <span class="kw">or</span> cusum_hit <span class="kw">or</span> gm_hit)
<span class="com"># If stress, scale portfolio weights down toward cash (--risk_scale)</span>
</pre>
  <p>
    Two things to notice:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The GMM doesn't predict returns. It predicts which of K hidden regimes today
      belongs to. The regime then <em>gates</em> the return-prediction model's
      output — a classic "decoupled signal + decoupled risk" architecture.
    </li>
    <li>
      Four independent stress detectors are OR-ed together: vol filter, drawdown
      filter, CUSUM change-point test, and the GMM regime probability. This is
      <strong>ensembling at the regime level</strong>, not the prediction level.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Repo honesty:</strong> the regime overlay is impressive scaffolding, but
    none of the saved model bundles in <code>models/</code> were trained with the
    full risk overlay turned on. The headline ~27% annualized number comes from the
    base GBM. Treat the HMM/CUSUM/etc. as <em>available knobs</em> rather than
    proven contributors until you ablate them yourself.
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §6 OFTEN CONFUSED WITH ===================== -->
  <h2>6. Often confused with</h2>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Predicting a return vs predicting a price</h4>
      <p>
        Always predict <strong>returns</strong>, not <strong>prices</strong>. Prices
        are non-stationary (a $200 AAPL is not comparable to a $100 AAPL), have
        wildly different scales across tickers, and are dominated by their long-run
        drift. Returns are stationary-enough, comparable, and zero-mean.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Cross-sectional vs time-series prediction</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Cross-sectional</strong> = "given many assets on day T, rank them."
        <strong>Time-series</strong> = "given one asset, forecast its next value."
        The repo's GBM is cross-sectional in spirit (top-N over all tickers each day);
        a per-ticker LSTM would be time-series.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Forecasting vs filtering</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Forecasting</strong>: estimate a future value (next return).
        <strong>Filtering</strong>: estimate a present hidden state from past
        observations (HMM regime "is today calm or stressed?"). The GMM overlay
        is filtering, not forecasting.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Point estimate vs distributional forecast</h4>
      <p>
        A <strong>point estimate</strong> says "expected return = 0.4%." A
        <strong>distributional forecast</strong> says "expected = 0.4%, std = 1.8%,
        95% interval [−2.6%, 3.4%]." Risk-aware sizing wants the latter; the repo
        works around having only point estimates by computing covariance separately
        in <code>predict_next.py</code>.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Walk-forward retrain vs online learning</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Walk-forward retrain</strong>: stop, refit on a window, resume.
        <strong>Online learning</strong>: model updates after every observation
        (incremental SGD, Bayesian update). The repo's <code>rolling_retrain</code>
        is the former — periodic batch refit, not true online.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>In-sample vs out-of-sample R²</h4>
      <p>
        Both numbers will be reported by any honest backtest. In-sample R² is
        always optimistic — it's the score on the rows the model has already seen.
        Out-of-sample R² is the only one anyone should trust. Look for "test_preds"
        in this repo's <code>--save_preds</code> output.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

