  <h2>7. Practice — click an answer for instant feedback</h2>
  <p class="small">Options shuffle on every page load. Read each one — distractors teach too.</p>

  <div class="question" data-question>
    <p class="q">Q1. <code>paper_trading_sim.py</code> trains <em>one</em> <code>GradientBoostingRegressor</code> on the stacked dataset from all tickers, dropping the ticker column from the features. What is the implicit assumption?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="No — yfinance gives daily OHLCV per ticker, and each ticker has its own per-row features. Pooling is a modeling decision, not an artefact of the data source.">That yfinance can't return per-ticker data, only stacked panels.</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Right. Without the ticker column, the model treats every row identically — it learns a single feature → return mapping that's supposed to apply to any ticker. If small-cap biotech behaves nothing like mega-cap tech, that assumption breaks down and a real production setup would bucket by sector or market cap first.">That the relationship between technical features and next-day return is roughly universal across tickers — i.e. it doesn't depend on which company you're looking at.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Dropping the column is what enforces the assumption — but the assumption itself is about feature-return universality, not about overfitting prevention.">That including the ticker column would cause overfitting on a categorical variable.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="There's no embedding being learned here. GradientBoostingRegressor with default settings doesn't do learned embeddings of any kind.">That the model will learn its own ticker embedding from the numeric features.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="question" data-question>
    <p class="q">Q2. What does the <code>GaussianMixture</code> block in <code>walkforward_sim.py</code> actually predict?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="No — GaussianMixture is unsupervised here, fit on a window of recent returns. It doesn't see fwd_ret_1d at all and never predicts return magnitudes.">The next day's return magnitude.</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct. GMM is a *filtering* model — given recent returns, it estimates the probability that today belongs to each of K Gaussian components, one of which is treated as the 'high-vol / stress' component. The output is a state probability, not a return.">The probability that the most recent day belongs to a 'high-vol' / 'stress' latent component, used to gate portfolio exposure.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="GBM here means Gradient Boosting; the GaussianMixture (GMM) is a separate, smaller model used only for the risk overlay, not as the prediction model.">The same thing as the GradientBoostingRegressor — it's an ensemble alternative.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="The GMM looks at past returns, not future ones. Forecasting tomorrow's volatility is a different (forecasting) problem; the GMM does filtering of the present hidden state.">Tomorrow's volatility level as a forecast.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="question" data-question>
    <p class="q">Q3. You decide to add a target that predicts "will the stock move more than 5% tomorrow?" as a binary 0/1. What's the most accurate description of what you've just changed?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="That's adaptivity (axis 5). You haven't touched retrain frequency. You changed what the model predicts.">You've changed the adaptivity axis — the model will now retrain more often.</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Right. You moved from a continuous regression target (return magnitude) to a binary classification target (large move yes/no). That cascades: you'd switch from GradientBoostingRegressor to GradientBoostingClassifier, change the loss to log-loss, and your downstream code can no longer rank by predicted magnitude — only by predicted probability of the event.">You've changed the target axis from continuous regression to binary classification, which cascades into a different loss function and a different downstream allocation step.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="No — pooling describes whether you have one model per ticker or one shared. Adding a different target doesn't change that.">You've changed the pooling axis from cross-sectional to per-asset.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Output shape distinguishes point estimates from distributional forecasts; both regression and classification output point estimates of different things. The bigger change is target type, not output shape.">You've changed the output-shape axis from point to distributional.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="question" data-question>
    <p class="q">Q4. Why does <code>build_features</code> in <code>paper_trading_sim.py</code> use <code>pct_change</code> (returns) rather than the raw close price as the target?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="That's true but secondary. The deeper reason is that returns are roughly stationary while prices are not — a $50 AAPL and a $250 AAPL are not directly comparable.">Because returns are smaller numbers, so the model converges faster.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Returns have heavier tails than a Gaussian (kurtosis is high). It's not the normality that justifies using returns — it's stationarity and cross-asset comparability.">Because daily returns are normally distributed, but prices aren't.</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct. Prices are non-stationary (their mean drifts with the long-run trend), and a $50 AAPL is a completely different scale from a $250 AAPL or a $10 penny stock. Returns are roughly stationary, comparable across tickers, and centered near zero — properties a pooled cross-sectional model relies on.">Because prices are non-stationary and not comparable across tickers; returns are roughly stationary and comparable, which the cross-sectional pooling assumption requires.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="It's not a library limitation — sklearn happily fits on price-like targets. The reason to avoid them is statistical, not technical.">Because <code>GradientBoostingRegressor</code> rejects targets with non-zero mean.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="question" data-question>
    <p class="q">Q5. A colleague proposes replacing the GBM with a time-series foundation model (TimesFM or Chronos) for daily next-day prediction. Best response?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Pretrained ≠ better on tabular alpha. On the academic benchmarks they shine; on equity cross-sectional return prediction, a tuned GBM is still hard to beat. Recent papers (Re-Visiting TSFMs in Finance, 2025) document this.">"Great — these models are pretrained on billions of points, so they'll outperform our tiny dataset.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="No — TimesFM/Chronos run on standard CPU/GPU. The relevant constraint here isn't compute; it's whether the model's strengths match the problem.">"Won't work — those models can't run on consumer hardware.</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Right. Foundation models like TimesFM, Chronos, and Kronos shine on rich sequential inputs with little fine-tuning, but on tabular daily-bar features in a cross-sectional ranking task — exactly what this repo does — well-tuned gradient boosting is still the workhorse. Worth experimenting on a side branch, not worth replacing the proven path.">"Worth a side experiment but not a replacement — on tabular daily-bar features with cross-sectional ranking, well-tuned GBM is still hard to beat per recent finance benchmarks of TSFMs.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="The repo's structure has nothing to do with whether TSFMs would win. They'd integrate fine; the open question is predictive performance, not plumbing.">"Impossible — our pipeline is tabular, and these models only accept text.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §8 SCENARIOS ===================== -->
  <h2>8. Check your understanding (scenarios)</h2>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S1. You add a small-cap biotech to your <code>tickers.txt</code> alongside the existing large-cap tech names. The backtest's Sharpe drops noticeably. What's the most likely modeling-level cause?</summary>
    <p>
      The pooled model is now averaging across two regimes — small-cap biotech and
      large-cap tech — that have genuinely different relationships between
      technical features and next-day return (biotech is event-driven; tech is
      momentum-driven). The cross-sectional assumption breaks. Fixes: bucket the
      universe by sector or market cap and train one model per bucket, or add
      categorical/sector features back into the input. The repo doesn't bucket
      today — this is a real, fixable gap.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S2. You want to add a confidence interval to each prediction so <code>predict_next.py</code> can size positions smaller when the model is uncertain. What axis are you changing, and what's a clean way to do it without abandoning the current GBM?</summary>
    <p>
      That's the <strong>output-shape axis</strong> — moving from point estimate to
      distributional forecast. Clean ways: (a) train two GBMs with quantile loss
      at the 10th and 90th percentiles to bracket the prediction (sklearn supports
      <code>loss='quantile'</code> via the <code>HistGradientBoostingRegressor</code>); (b)
      compute residual standard deviation on a held-out validation slice and use
      that as a constant uncertainty; (c) bootstrap-ensemble many GBMs and use the
      empirical spread. Option (a) is the simplest principled choice and lives
      within the existing toolchain.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S3. <code>walkforward_sim.py</code> with <code>--rolling_retrain --retrain_freq monthly</code> beats the train-once mode of <code>paper_trading_sim.py</code> on 2020–2024 by a wide margin. Why isn't rolling retrain just always the right choice?</summary>
    <p>
      Two reasons. First, <em>compute</em>: every retrain costs a full GBM fit;
      monthly is fine, daily is expensive. Second, and more important,
      <em>statistical noise</em>: a too-frequent retrain on a too-short window will
      overfit the most recent market microstructure and whiplash. The default
      <code>--train_window_days 756</code> (~3 years) and monthly retrain is a
      defensible bias-variance compromise — but it's a compromise, not a free win.
      Always-train-on-everything (no rolling window) drifts; always-retrain-on-the-
      last-month chases noise. The sweet spot is asset- and regime-dependent.
    </p>
  </details>

  <p class="footer-note">
    Next lesson candidates: <strong>(a) Walk-forward backtesting + look-ahead bias</strong>
    formalized against <code>walkforward_sim.py</code> internals, or
    <strong>(b) Mean-variance portfolio optimization</strong> grounded in
    <code>predict_next.py:meanvar_long_only</code>. Lesson 0005's scenarios poked at both.
  </p>

